From: Anaconda Recipes
Subject: [PATCH] Make is_compatible() iteration tolerant of exceptions

deepspeed/git_version_info.py iterates ALL_OPS and calls each builder's
is_compatible(). Several CUDA op_builders (fp_quantizer, transformer_inference,
inference_core_ops, ragged_ops, evoformer_attn, ...) call installed_cuda_version()
inside that branch without guarding against the MissingCUDAException it raises
when CUDA_HOME is None. This breaks `import deepspeed` at import time on any GPU
machine that has the CUDA runtime libs (so torch.cuda.is_available() returns True)
but no CUDA toolkit / nvcc installed.

Wrap the iteration in a try/except so any single op's is_compatible failure
doesn't abort the import — falling back to "not compatible" is the same outcome
as the existing cuda_okay = False branches inside the op_builders.

--- a/deepspeed/git_version_info.py
+++ b/deepspeed/git_version_info.py
@@ -27,5 +27,8 @@
 compatible_ops = dict.fromkeys(ALL_OPS.keys(), False)
 for op_name, builder in ALL_OPS.items():
-    op_compatible = builder.is_compatible()
+    try:
+        op_compatible = builder.is_compatible()
+    except Exception:
+        op_compatible = False
     compatible_ops[op_name] = op_compatible
     compatible_ops["deepspeed_not_implemented"] = False
